# Backend Stack — C# / ASP.NET Core 8

Always implement the backend with **C#** and **ASP.NET Core 8** (.NET 8). Do not use Node.js, TypeScript, Python, or other runtimes for server-side code in this project.

## Defaults

- Target framework: `net8.0`
- Web API: ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs or controllers (match existing project structure)
- Place backend code under `src/` (e.g. `src/OrderOps.Api/` or similar)
- Use built-in DI, `IHostApplicationBuilder` / `WebApplication`, and standard middleware
- Prefer EF Core 8 for PostgreSQL data access when a database layer is needed

## When implementing features

- REST endpoints: ASP.NET Core Web API with proper HTTP status codes and JSON serialization
- Background/async work: `IHostedService`, `BackgroundService`, or Redis-backed queues as appropriate
- Real-time (WebSocket/SSE): ASP.NET Core SignalR or native SSE endpoints
- Validation: Data annotations, FluentValidation, or `IValidatableObject` — not ad-hoc checks in controllers only

## Examples

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — ASP.NET Core 8 Web API project
// OrderOps.Api.csproj: <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();
var app = builder.Build();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — Node/Express or other non-.NET backend
// app.get('/api/orders', ...)
```

## Out of scope for this rule

Frontend remains **React + TypeScript** unless the user explicitly requests otherwise.
